WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.318 --> 00:00:02.818
(soft music)

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.250
- So when I was invited to give a tutorial

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.870
or the lecture at the summer school,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.150
I was told to talk about
the modern architectures

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:12.983
in deep learning,

00:00:12.983 --> 00:00:16.930
and I realize that in
fact, surprisingly so

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.690
the network neural net architecture

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.860
hasn't really evolved that
much ever since 2017 or 2018

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.840
when we started to use
attention everywhere.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.940
Instead, I thought, okay,
let's try to think of a case

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.440
where we can view many
different, let's say perspectives

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.360
into training deep neural networks

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.150
from a perspective of the
neural network architecture.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.810
And then what would be the
best way to demonstrate this?

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.330
I thought was the hyperparameter tuning,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.400
which is another way
to look at optimization

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.640
or the network architecture,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.700
but from a one level up.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.210
So today's lecture is going to be

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.510
about the online hyperparameter tuning,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.890
and I want it to be as
interactive as possible.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.490
I understand that online
setup is very difficult

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.730
for any lecture to be interactive,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.398
but I do have a recording
of this particular lecture,

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.880
it's not going to be exactly the same,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.260
that I can share with
all the students later

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.410
so that they can review it.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.880
So if you have any
questions as I go about,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.260
because I'm going to cover a
lot of background materials,

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.040
do ask and then Sarah will be able

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.440
to relay the questions for me,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.140
and then I can answer all of them.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.440
So when I think about optimization

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.273
or the network architecture

00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:30.220
or whatever you want in deep learning,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.350
I always imagine four different stages.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.410
One is preparation because
at the end of the day,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.743
what is the most important thing?

00:01:35.743 --> 00:01:38.370
It's to prepare data properly

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.170
and then prepare what kinds of models

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.003
that we are going to use,

00:01:41.003 --> 00:01:42.356
or what kinds of optimization algorithms

00:01:42.356 --> 00:01:44.020
we are going to use properly.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.930
And then after the preparation is done,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.750
we actually do have a two
stages of optimization.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.040
In the first stage, which we
often refer to as training,

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.930
we pick one particular model architecture,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:55.820
network architecture,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.490
or the one particular
hyper-parameter optimization,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.900
or I would just refer to
all of them collectively

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.480
as a hypothesis set,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.550
and then trying to find a solution

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:06.170
within this hypothesis set,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.290
that's going to have the
minimum average training loss.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.630
And then that's the second
stage of the training.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.310
And then after that is done,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.100
of course it was an optimization problem.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.130
In the third stage

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.580
that is often referred
to as model selection,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.880
we solve yet another optimization problem.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.490
Where we're going to look
at multiple hypothesis sets

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.127
and there could be as many
hypotheses as you want.

00:02:26.127 --> 00:02:29.380
And we trying to find the
one of which the solution

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.710
corresponds to the minimum validation loss

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.493
so the average validation loss.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.570
After the model selection is done,

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.770
we are done with the optimization.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.390
and then we enter the stage,

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.320
the last stage of reporting.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.360
So we need to report or tell other people

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.680
who are going to use our model,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.320
how well this model is going to work.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.153
And then for that,

00:02:48.153 --> 00:02:51.220
we are going to simply
compute the average test loss

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.670
and then report it
together with our model.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.150
And today I'm going to
focus on these two stages,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.920
the stage two and the stage three

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.520
that are all about optimization.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.300
Now, training, most of
you already know yourself

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.690
and that you probably
have learned about it

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.770
from the three earlier
lectures of this summer school.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:10.603
For training,

00:03:10.603 --> 00:03:13.930
we almost exclusively use
stochastic gradient descent

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.332
or a variant of stochastic
gradient descent.

00:03:17.332 --> 00:03:19.080
And in stochastic gradient descent,

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.400
we're trying to find the classifier

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.680
that is parametrized with theta

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.480
which refers to collection
of the parameters.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.220
So in the case of the neural network,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.210
we have a very large neural network.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.370
And then we have all those weight values.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.210
And then all of those weight values

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.450
are going to be referred
to collectively as theta.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.463
And then we often need

00:03:38.463 --> 00:03:41.520
a differentiable per
example loss function, L.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.230
So for each of the example
in the training set,

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.310
we want to know how well this
classifier's prediction is

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.480
with respect to the ground truth, Y?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.120
And then we want this
per-example loss function

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.890
together with a classifier
to be differentiable

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:58.230
with respect to the
parameters of this network.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.110
So we don't care whether
it's differentiable

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.200
with respect to the ground
truth, label Y or the input X,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.620
but with respect to the parameters

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.163
we want it to be jointly differentiable.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.460
And we often need to have a
differentiable regularizer

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:15.470
such as a weight or what
not that's going to ensure

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.040
that our hypothesis set is
properly regularized or limited

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.860
so that we are able to avoid
overfitting to a training set

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.893
without generalizing to a new example.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.850
And why is this stochastic
gradient descent?

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.630
Because if you have noticed,

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.570
then in fact computing this loss function

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.410
or the gradient of this loss function,

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.800
entire loss function with
respect to the parameters

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.780
is extremely expensive.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:39.892
What do I mean by that?

00:04:39.892 --> 00:04:43.870
We need to evaluate how
good this classifier is

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.360
for every single training example.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.350
Every time we compute the
gradient of this loss function,

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.650
and this is simply not scalable

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.270
in particular, in this
era of so-called big data.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.470
We have data sets that has
often let's say millions,

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.260
if not billions of instances.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.470
And if you have to go through
billion different examples,

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.540
every time we make a tiny
update to the parameters,

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.960
that's not really what we want to do

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.890
nor we can afford to do so.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.210
So instead we have to be a bit stochastic,

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.930
and then say that we will use

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.250
a very small mini batch at a time

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.140
and then compute the gradient

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.170
of this mini batch loss function.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.270
To do so, all we need to do
is evaluate this function

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.650
or the neural network,

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.687
as many times as there are
examples in this mini batch.

00:05:26.687 --> 00:05:29.280
And this mini batch size
can be as small as one,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.170
to sometimes several thousand only.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.180
And then compared to the
say, millions or billions

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.013
that we are talking about

00:05:34.013 --> 00:05:36.240
over the size of the
training set we talked about

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.270
is pretty reasonable.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.800
We can manage it, right?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.770
And then when we do so, we need to think

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.970
about what kinds of
hyper-parameters there are.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.367
So these such as the learning rate

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:48.840
and regularization coefficient,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.000
I'm going to refer to them

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.990
as the optimization hyper-parameters

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.840
because these hyper-parameters
directly influence

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.630
how the optimization happens.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:58.840
Of course, there are many
different hyper-parameters

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.250
such as the size of the network,

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.380
like the width of each of the layers

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.290
that's of the network and so on.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.560
But, my sense is that
we just make the network

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.340
as large as we can considering
the memory requirement

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.740
and the computer requirement.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.350
And then all the matters

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:15.890
is to find the good hyper-parameters

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.523
for the optimization procedure.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.080
Now, one really interesting thing.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.157
One really interesting thing

00:06:24.157 --> 00:06:25.740
about stochastic gradient descent

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.630
in this optimization hyper-parameters

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:28.760
is that they turned out

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.900
that the choice of the
optimization hyper-parameters

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.710
turned out to influence
the solution that we find

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.310
very, very significantly.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.143
What that means

00:06:39.143 --> 00:06:41.730
is that even though our
loss function is fixed,

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.790
so our loss function is given

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.960
depending on what kind of
hyper-parameters we use

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.560
for optimization.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.420
We may not be able to
reach some of the solutions

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:55.010
while we end up at some
certain sets of the solutions

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.730
that are reachable.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:57.750
So for instance,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.830
if you start with a
very high learning rate,

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:01.663
at the beginning,

00:07:01.663 --> 00:07:05.040
then we might end up with a
well generalizing solutions.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.120
However, if you start with
a very low learning rate,

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.600
we can always know what
kind of scheduling we set,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.290
we are very likely to end up

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.500
with the non generalizing solutions.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.859
And then this kind of observation

00:07:16.859 --> 00:07:21.510
has been made multiple times
over the past few years.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.400
And then some have explained it

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.437
from the perspective of so-called
implicit regularization,

00:07:26.437 --> 00:07:28.540
and then some others have approached it

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.930
from the lens of the importance

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.940
of the early stage of learning.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.520
But I don't really care.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.850
I don't really care what
the underlying causes are.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.710
But the important thing to notice here

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.800
is that the optimization hyper-parameters,

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:42.750
such as the learning rate,

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:46.440
how you set them will change
what kinds of solutions

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:47.310
you're going to find

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.240
or what kind of neural net

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.190
you're going to end up with at the end.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.440
So what that means is that assuming

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.180
that we going to use as
large neural net as possible,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:59.350
be it transformers, be it compromise,

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.150
or be it multi-layer perception,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:02.990
with all those fully connected layers,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.280
if you use a large network
and then all that remains

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:08.400
is for us to choose a good set

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.220
of optimization hyper-parameters

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.140
such as learning rate,
regularization coefficients

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.470
and whatnot.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:15.480
And then to do so,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.770
we now enter the third stage
that is model selection.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:19.760
And in model selection,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.512
which is often solved as
a blackbox optimization.

00:08:22.512 --> 00:08:24.550
What we do is we're going to go through

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:27.920
all possible optimization hyper-parameters

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.750
and then for each setup,

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:33.120
we are going to train
this neural net entirely,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.760
take the final solution,

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.870
and then evaluate this final
solution on a validation set

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.603
that is separate from the training set.

00:08:39.603 --> 00:08:43.150
It's very important to use
a separate validation set

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.280
that is not training set.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.170
And once we evaluate it

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.263
each and every optimization
hyper-parameters,

00:08:50.263 --> 00:08:51.160
we are going to pick one

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.940
that has the lowest validation loss.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.320
And this is often solved
as blackbox optimization,

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.153
as I said earlier,

00:08:57.153 --> 00:09:01.440
because it's just so expensive
to train the entire model.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.320
So that is the evaluation,

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:06.273
one time evaluation of this loss function,

00:09:06.273 --> 00:09:08.760
that is a validation loss
function is very expensive

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.680
because every time we evaluate

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:11.513
this validation loss function,

00:09:11.513 --> 00:09:13.890
we need to train the
entire neural network.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.400
And then as I said earlier,

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.093
we are assuming that we're
going to use as accurate

00:09:17.093 --> 00:09:18.263
on that as we can.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.520
So what are some of
the standard approaches

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.190
to this model selection or
the black box optimization

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:27.970
that is used to choose

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:30.650
the right optimization hyper-parameters?

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.990
The first one you can
imagine is grid search.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:34.010
We're going to simply look

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.080
at all possible optimization
hyper-parameters

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:38.700
make a grid in this space

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:40.770
of the optimization hyper-parameters

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:45.060
and then evaluate every
possible uniformly space point

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.980
in this space of the
optimization hyper-parameters.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.960
It's quite standard,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.730
you know that because it's
available in scikit-learn.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:53.640
Unfortunately it does not scale well

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.070
with the number of hyper-parameters

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.240
because of the curse of dimensionality.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.010
We learned about curse of dimensionality

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.780
in introduction to machine learning,

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.300
condemnatory optimization,
all of those places.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:05.250
And then you have this

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.780
where we really, really literally run

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:10.110
into the curse of
dimensionality in practice.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.620
The number of the uniformly spaced points

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:13.640
that we need to evaluate

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.320
in order to exactly solve this grid search

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:18.000
grows exponentially with respect

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.020
to a number of optimization
hyper-parameters.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.050
So this is not really what we want to use.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.950
And the interesting thing
is that it turned out

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.310
one of the simplest possible solutions

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.840
to address this issue of
the curse of dimensionality

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.450
has been to simply use a random search.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:36.210
So we're going to select

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.820
a random set of
hyper-parameters drawn uniformly

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.710
or they look uniformly from
this hyper-parameters space,

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.440
and then evaluate all
those randomly drawn points

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.040
and choose the one that has
the lowest validation loss.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.210
Now to be strict, there's
absolutely no reason

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.890
why this should work
better than grid search

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:55.690
without assuming a certain thing

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.310
about the underlying loss function itself.

00:10:58.310 --> 00:10:59.930
However, in practice,

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.650
it turned out that for
training neural nets

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.670
or in selecting the hyper-parameters

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.300
for when you are training neural nets,

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:07.210
this one works pretty well.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.690
This is perhaps one of
the most cited papers

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.870
in the field of deep learning,

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:15.443
that is Bergstra &amp; Bengio,
2012 that came out of Montreal.

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:18.200
Of course we can do better.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.057
So whenever you talk to people
and then they tell you that,

00:11:21.057 --> 00:11:24.450
"Yo, we are solving this
difficult optimization problem

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.960
with random search or the grid search."

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.017
You can always tell them that,

00:11:28.017 --> 00:11:29.800
"Yo, we can solve better."

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.960
And then one way in which
we can solve better,

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:34.140
this kind of black box
optimization problem

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.840
is so-called sequential
model-based optimization.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:41.610
So instead of trying to
evaluate all the points

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:44.120
and then just use those
point-wise information

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:46.400
that we get for every evaluation,

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.770
in sequential model-based optimization,

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.680
we're trying to fit a model

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:52.570
that's going to look very similar

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:56.350
to the underlying loss function
validation loss function,

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.330
using as few a number of evaluation

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.043
or the points as possible.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:04.440
Now what is the
requirements for this model?

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.359
This model shouldn't just tell us

00:12:06.359 --> 00:12:09.480
how each of the points should evaluate to

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.730
or that correspond to the
certain validation loss.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:14.510
But it should also tell
us given any other point

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.390
what the uncertainty is.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.730
So we want to know what are the points

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.100
in this hyper-parameters space

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:24.290
for which we know the
actual validation loss of.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.930
And also we want to know
how certain our knowledge

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.830
about this validation loss is.

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:32.130
And then using this
two information that is

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.470
what is the average
validation loss I can expect

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:35.980
from a particular point

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:38.830
and how certain my prediction

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.580
of this average validation
loss is at the point.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.140
Now I can balance them in order

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.680
to explore this space
of the hyper-parameters

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.460
or the optimization
hyper-parameters to be specific

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.573
much better, or the much more efficient.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:54.550
Now, of course I can go into the details

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:55.383
about the whole thing,

00:12:55.383 --> 00:12:58.550
but that's going to take up
probably multiple lectures.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.970
So my suggestion is to go look
at the Brochu et al, 2010,

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.360
and then that's a pretty
accessible tutorial

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:05.670
that I really enjoyed reading

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.420
to learn about the sequential
model-based optimization.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.130
Now in the context of machine
learning or deep learning,

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:17.000
or even more specifically,
optimization and deep learning

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.260
Bayesian optimization
with Gaussian Process

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.520
has been one of the de facto standard

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:25.400
in such a sequential
model-based optimization

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:26.233
for model selection

00:13:26.233 --> 00:13:28.470
or the optimization
hyper-parameter selection.

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:32.520
This was put forward as a
proposal in 2015 by Snoek et al

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.520
and it's a very, a beautiful
way to solve this problem.

00:13:37.757 --> 00:13:41.480
And so everything that all the
machine learning people love

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:42.400
kind of come in.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.000
There's a Gaussian process,

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.780
there's a Bayesian, let's say treatment,

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.300
there is the active learning component

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.580
and there's an uncertainty quantification,

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.020
it's pretty beautiful.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.880
And then it's become pretty
much a defacto standard

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.340
in particular last year,
if I remember correctly,

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.220
there was a competition in Europe

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.870
about the blackbox optimization,

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:03.700
Bayesian optimization
with Gaussian process

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:07.660
was one of the winning entries,
if not the winning entry.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.960
And then you can use it
quite easily nowadays,

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.540
you can just use the scikit-optimize

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.540
and then together with the scikit-learn

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:15.500
and then it's going to find

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.603
all those nice hyper-parameters for you.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:20.590
Now, that was good.

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:23.320
But what is the one thing
that all these people

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.360
who have been teaching
and who are going to teach

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:26.677
at this deep learning

00:14:26.677 --> 00:14:29.600
and reinforcement learning
summer school love

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:32.260
is gradient-based optimization.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:33.730
And then unfortunately what we noticed

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:34.960
was that the training part,

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:38.210
so that the half of the
optimization in deep learning,

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.640
we now know how to
solve it very, very well

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:41.910
with the gradient-based tuning

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.560
or the gradient-based optimization,

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:45.930
but the other half, the
model selection part,

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:46.963
we have really haven't used

00:14:46.963 --> 00:14:49.570
a gradient-based optimization there.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.100
And then it's been always treated

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.447
as a blackbox optimization.

00:14:53.447 --> 00:14:56.190
Obviously, because we all love it,

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:59.510
people have worked on gradient-based
hyper-parameter tuning

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:00.650
for some time.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:04.360
And I'm going to go
through some of these now.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:07.270
But let's see, oh, I see there is a Q&amp;A.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.900
Let's see, there are
a couple of questions.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:10.567
Let me try to answer
them before I move on,

00:15:10.567 --> 00:15:12.300
because this is a perfect time

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.040
to answer some of the questions.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.100
So let's see.

