00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:51.113
The second question is by our Arfan Miahi.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:54.700
Does Bayesian optimization scale well

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.010
with a number of hyper-parameters?

00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:00.010
So no blackbox optimization algorithms

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:03.480
without any assumptions of
the underlying function,

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.070
will scale it well with a
number of hyper-parameters

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:08.980
because you can always come
up with a loss function

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.280
that just cannot be solved well

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.290
without knowing the curvature information.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.940
But at least for the case

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.640
of the hyper-parameter tuning and whatnot,

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:21.160
at least personally I've
had a good experience

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:24.863
using Bayesian optimization for
tuning many hyper-parameters

00:16:24.863 --> 00:16:27.740
like the layer wise
learning rate and whatnot.

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:31.100
Of course, I believe there
will be few other people

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:34.210
at the summer school who
are much more experts

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:35.930
in Bayesian optimization than I am,

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.320
so you should ask them about it.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:39.153
Let me see.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.170
So let's see, one last question

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:43.650
before I move on to the gradient-based

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.420
hyper-parameter tuning by
the anonymous attendee.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.320
Could you give an example of
acquisition function in SMBL?

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:51.510
So let's say

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:53.780
so there are a lot of the
acquisition functions,

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.760
and then it turns out that the choosing

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.570
or designing the
acquisition function itself

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.940
is a bit more of an art than science.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:03.210
But one of the things
that people have used,

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:05.400
or the kind of standard
one that everyone learns

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.990
is the upper competence bound,

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.170
or the lower competence,

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.260
I don't know what's the
right way to say it.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:13.680
But that is that the, I
want to choose a point

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.680
that's going to have a very low loss,

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:19.510
but at the same time has
a high enough uncertainty

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:22.460
so that I hit the balance
between exploitation.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.840
That is find the low validation loss,

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:28.230
but also I try the points
that I'm not entirely sure yet

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:30.230
whether there is a good solution or not.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:33.350
So this one is like the
whole, let's say lectures,

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:34.183
that can be done.

00:17:34.183 --> 00:17:38.510
And then obviously I'm not
the most qualified one to do.

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:40.280
So, if the ask, let's say Sarah

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:43.330
and many others who are at
the summer school, they know.

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.110
Oh, and then there is one short question.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:47.050
I'm going to answer this one

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:48.670
before I move on to the gradient-based

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:49.830
hyper-parameter tuning.

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:51.900
The question is can the mini batch size

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:53.580
be interpreted it as a hyper-parameter?

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.480
Yes, indeed, it can be
interpreted as a hyper-parameter,

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:58.360
but just like the network architecture,

00:17:58.360 --> 00:17:59.730
or the size of the network.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:02.060
I do believe that it's often a good idea

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:06.480
to make the neural network
as large as possible,

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:07.900
as much as you can afford

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:09.540
and then use a mini batch size

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:12.330
that again, you can
afford, as much as you can.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:17.330
Although, my sense is that
the size of the mini batch

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:20.870
shouldn't matter that much.

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:23.100
I know that that's not true in practice,

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.580
especially when we train
transformers and whatnot.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.690
I almost feel like there's just a issue

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:30.540
with the network architecture
or the loss function itself

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:32.660
rather than the size of the mini batch.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:35.944
But this is a very much open question.

00:18:35.944 --> 00:18:37.144
Thanks for the question.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:43.660
And then thanks, Adriana, who
actually answered my question,

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.390
the first question on my behalf.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:50.370
So I'm going to read out the
title of the Brochu et al.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:51.917
So it was a paper titled,

00:18:51.917 --> 00:18:54.080
"A Tutorial on Bayesian Optimization

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.680
of Expensive Cost
Functions with Application

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:57.930
to Active User Modeling

00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.170
and Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning"

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:03.593
and was authored by Brochu,
Cora and de Freitas.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:07.840
So if you think about this
model selection and learning,

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.010
you notice that these stages two and three

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:12.200
can be just merged into a single

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:13.970
bilevel optimization problem.

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:14.803
So there is an outer optimization,

00:19:14.803 --> 00:19:17.180
that is the usual model selection,

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.320
but what we notice here

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:22.210
is that the one particular
part of this outer optimization

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:24.800
there is the theta ahead of the lambda

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.000
is in fact, the solution, a solution

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.090
from solving the inner
optimization problem

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.330
that is training a neural network.

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:33.670
So I'm going to just refer to it

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:35.563
as bi-level optimization for now.

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:40.550
The reason why, I refer to
it as bi-level optimization

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:43.090
just for now is that it
turned out in practice,

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:45.390
it's actually not a bi-level optimization.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.320
So let's imagine the idea,

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.960
how we are solving this
inner optimization problem.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:52.870
That is to minimize the
average training loss

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:55.370
with respect to the parameters
of the neural network

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:56.510
that is theta.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:58.870
So we're going to start
initializing this theta

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:02.940
with a theta zero set drawn
from a sum distribution

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:04.700
that we think are supposed to be good

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.830
for initializing these neural networks.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:08.950
There are so many of
them that are being done.

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:11.590
Like you probably have heard

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:13.710
about the globe initialization,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:17.010
you probably have heard about
the whole initialization,

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.660
you probably have heard of how
PI torch initialize things,

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:22.100
TensorFlow initialize things.

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:24.000
And then somehow everyone
has their own way

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.150
of initializing these parameters.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.090
Once the initialization is done,

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.760
we're going to use
stochastic gradient descent,

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:35.070
or experiments such as Adam in
order to update this progress

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:36.070
little by middle.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:39.150
So we're going to compute theta
T given theta T minus one,

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:41.040
and this update direction that is computed

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.510
using a mini batch at time step, T

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:46.030
and the parameters from
the previous time step,

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:47.740
theta T minus one.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:50.393
And after sometime, after sometime,

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:55.090
even if the optimization has
not been solved perfectly,

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:56.810
we are going to just stop.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:59.450
We often stop it based
on various criteria,

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.870
including the early stopping
based on the validation loss,

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:04.290
or we're going to just stop
it because we ran out of time

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:06.060
or we are going to stop it

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.610
because we notice that the
change in the loss value

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:13.610
is within some tolerance
that we predefined earlier.

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.120
So what that means is that
we actually never find

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:18.490
the fixed point of this
optimization problem,

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:22.223
but we just stop it at some
point that is reasonable.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:27.470
And what we notice here is that with this,

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.920
we noticed that this
whole stochastic gradients

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.150
are actually differentiable
with respect to lambda,

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:34.560
that is the optimization hyper-parameter

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.940
So rather than solving the
inner optimization problem,

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:40.320
we could imagine that we
just have to write out

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:44.550
a very very long and complicated procedure

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:48.430
to extend the validation loss function.

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.610
And then this validation loss function

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:52.620
that includes this training procedure

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.180
of a finite number of steps

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:57.500
is differentiable not only with respect

00:21:57.500 --> 00:21:59.130
to the primary theta yourself,

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:00.940
but also differentiable with respect

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:02.300
to the optimization hyper-parameter

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:05.393
such as learning rate and
regularization coefficient.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:11.790
And what that means is that we
can now treat the whole thing

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:14.450
as a single optimization problem.

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:15.780
So we just collect the outer

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:18.590
and look into one optimization problem.

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:22.710
As it was noticed by
Yoshua in 2002, not 2020,

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:26.593
there's a typo that I
just never get to fix it.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:30.710
So you collect these two
optimization procedures,

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:32.870
and then you get just
one optimization problem.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.334
Now, the problem with optimization problem

00:22:35.334 --> 00:22:38.520
is that this function, the loss value,

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.850
the validation loss we use

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:42.580
is extremely, extremely expensive.

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:45.200
You can imagine why it's expensive,

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:47.780
you train the entire neural network

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:50.590
for a finite number of time,
but that is reasonable.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:55.010
And then we're trying to
evaluate the final solution.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:56.480
So it is very expensive,

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.370
but still because the
whole procedure of training

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.220
is differentiable

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:03.700
with respect to the optimization
hyper-parameter, lambda.

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:06.100
We can always use the back propagation

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.220
and then use the
gradient-based optimization

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:12.530
in order to find the optimization
hyper-parameter setup

00:23:12.530 --> 00:23:14.893
then to minimize the validation loss.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.550
Unfortunately, it's very expensive,

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:20.550
not only due to the forward pass,

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:22.310
so that is that the training,

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:24.120
but because of back propagation

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.130
through this back propagation procedure

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:27.550
or the training procedure

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:30.850
requires computing multiple copies.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:34.090
In fact, as many copies
of the Hessian matrix

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:36.510
as the number of the updates we have taken

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.050
in training this neural network.

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:41.797
Now, obviously we don't need to compute

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:46.020
the Hessian matrix explicitly,

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:50.400
but all we need is a Hessian
matrix times some vector.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.020
That's the only thing that we need.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:53.897
Unfortunately, that is still expensive

00:23:53.897 --> 00:23:57.280
and we have to do that every
time we made one update

00:23:57.280 --> 00:23:58.483
to the neutral network.

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:03.270
So super duper expensive, you can imagine.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.470
And that is for one tiny update

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:09.060
to the optimization hyper-parameters.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:13.400
What that means is that we
need some kind of approximation

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.000
and then by doing so,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.880
we need to make some
appropriate trade-off.

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:19.840
So, three different approaches

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.400
have been proposed and tested.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.320
One way, is that you just say that,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:25.640
okay, we don't really care

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:28.400
about training the neural net all the way,

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:31.120
and then see how that neural net does

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.160
in terms of validation loss.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:33.993
We're going to simply say,

00:24:33.993 --> 00:24:37.130
all we need to do is look at
the few steps of training,

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:38.720
and then that's enough for us

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:43.720
to figure out what are good
optimization hyper-parameters.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:47.000
The second approach is relying

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.820
on so-called implicit functions theorem

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:51.040
which is conceptually similar

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:52.810
to truncated backdrop-through-backdrop.

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:54.720
That is that we're going to go all the way

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:58.640
until there is a convergence
or until the training is over.

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:00.270
And then we're going to assume that-

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:03.190
Well, at this point, we
are going to simply assume

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:06.190
that the training loss function
looks really, really nice.

00:25:06.190 --> 00:25:07.720
And then we are going to look

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:10.430
at only last one step or the few steps

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.700
in order to approximate
this training loss function,

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:15.380
and then use that
information to approximate

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:19.840
what the Jacobian of the final solution

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:22.940
with respect to the
optimization hyper-parameters

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:24.123
could have been.

00:25:24.123 --> 00:25:25.180
This one actually works well,

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:27.370
it was actually proposed
in 2000 by Yoshua Bengio.

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:30.500
And then this idea was revived in 2016.

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:34.760
And then this idea is used for
various purposes ever since.

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:36.150
And then the final one

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:38.650
is the reversible iterative optimization.

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:41.877
We give up on reducing the computation,

00:25:41.877 --> 00:25:43.850
but we can at least reduce the amount

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.430
of the memory that is needed.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:47.500
Because if you think
about the amount of memory

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:50.120
that is required in
order to go all the way

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:52.050
until the end of the training,

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:54.420
and then backdrop all the way back,

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.000
it's actually pretty gigantic.

00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:58.320
But then if the optimization algorithm

00:25:58.320 --> 00:25:59.840
was designed or selected,

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:02.500
so that are every step is reversible

00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:04.990
then you don't have to save
all those intermediate steps.

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:09.340
And that allows us to
compute this gradient,

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:10.820
backdrop-by-backdrop gradient

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:13.240
or the gradient of the gradient exactly

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.893
without having to suffer
from the memory overhead.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:19.840
And as you can imagine,

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:22.500
you can mix and match all
these different things.

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:24.770
In fact, this is a table

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:28.270
coming out of the Lorraine et
al 2020, my favorite table,

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:32.750
because it really summarizes
what people have tried.

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.940
And then what are the things that-

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.990
What kind of computation
complexity we have?

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.860
And then what are the
approximation that has been done?

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:41.610
This is really nice table

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:42.880
and then the paper is really nice.

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:45.260
I highly recommend you to read it.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:47.240
But what you notice in general

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:49.660
is that there is a clear trade-off

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:51.250
between the computational complexity

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.630
or even the computational tractability

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.320
and the quality of approximation.

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:57.960
So in order to get a good quality,

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:01.330
we often have to spend
a lot on computation,

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:02.800
including memory.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:05.310
And then if you want
to save on computation,

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:08.543
we often have to sacrifice
the quality of approximation.

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:13.950
And in that case, we wanted to see,

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:17.470
can we actually do better than this?

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:19.170
So this is what current practice is.

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:23.630
And then we want to see if you
can do it a bit differently

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:25.660
than what most of the people have done.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:27.850
Although it turned out
that the few other people

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:30.140
have done what I'm
going to tell you about,

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:31.930
but we'll get to that soon.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.230
Before that, let's see,
there is one question.

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:37.010
Oh, Charlie Gothier.

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.690
Okay, so can you post the
