Sorry about that.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.830
Another question from Arthur Miahi,

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:57.870
are any of these bi-level
optimization techniques

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:00.823
suitable for optimizing
the neural network?

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.805
Well, it's an interesting question.

00:28:06.805 --> 00:28:08.500
So it's difficult to tell

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:10.760
what it means to optimize
in your architecture.

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:14.970
If you're asking whether we
can use bi-level optimization

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:16.370
which is what we are doing.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:18.380
We have a separate
hyper-parameter selection

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.430
to optimize the neural net architecture?

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.203
Yes, that's what we all-

00:28:22.203 --> 00:28:25.270
I know people are working on quite heavily

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:27.900
and then that's what essentially
Cokley has been working on

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:29.460
at Google for the past four years,

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:31.100
trying to use various techniques

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:34.180
or trying out all possible
techniques that you can imagine

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.230
to find the best network architecture,

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:37.790
or the primitives they
are going to minimize

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:39.930
their validation loss function,

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:41.760
but it's a very difficult problem.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:42.733
The reason why it's difficult,

00:28:42.733 --> 00:28:47.350
this is a extremely high
dimensional computational space

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:49.310
that is a neural net architecture space.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:53.160
And then there is not a
clear kind of inductive bias

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.960
that we can rely on in order to tell

00:28:56.071 --> 00:28:57.500
what are the similar architectures

00:28:57.500 --> 00:28:59.500
in terms of the actual generalization.

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:01.440
If we knew that, we could
actually bake that in

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:04.524
as a heuristics for
solving this (indistinct)

00:29:04.524 --> 00:29:06.200
but when it comes to
neural net architecture,

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:09.110
I don't think we have a good intuition

00:29:09.110 --> 00:29:13.053
what those right metrics
are in this space.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:15.633
Let me see.

00:29:15.633 --> 00:29:17.900
Yes, you meant neural
architecture research.

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:19.480
Hopefully I answered that already.

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:20.320
And then final question,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.180
before we go into the
slightly newer thing,

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:26.320
by anonymous atendee,
can you please go back

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:29.530
to which method was the current
practice arrow pointing to?

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:31.090
Oh, I'm sorry about this.

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:33.650
So everything that I've
talked about so far

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:35.940
is the current practice.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:37.470
That's what everyone has been doing.

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:39.690
That's what everyone
has proposed to do so,

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:42.420
and then that's what a
lot people have tried

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.213
and are using every day.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:48.110
So instead, let's see
if we can actually think

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.840
about a different way to solve it.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:54.320
And then to do so, we're
going to think about,

00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:58.134
we want to talk about a different
perspective to think about

00:29:58.134 --> 00:29:59.143
what learning is.

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:03.420
So if you look at the inner optimization,

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.400
there is training based on the
stochastic gradient descent

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:07.980
what we can think of

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:11.250
is that it creates a computational graph.

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:14.680
So at every time step, T, we
start with a theta T minus one,

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:16.470
that is the previous parameters.

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:19.120
And then given this theta T minus one,

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:22.060
we're going to compute the update delta.

00:30:22.060 --> 00:30:26.610
And then this delta takes
this input, theta T minus one,

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:28.820
as well as the mini batch.

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:31.680
A mini batch of examples
of time steps, T, Bt

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:35.660
and this whole function
delta is parameterized

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:38.203
using this optimization
hyper-parameter, lambda.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:44.450
Once this delta is computed,
we are going to add this delta.

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:46.850
So the update direction
to the theta T minus one

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.480
in order to result in theta T.

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.240
And then this whole procedure

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:54.073
happens over and over and over.

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:57.040
And then where do we start?

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:59.683
We start from the initial
parameters of theta zero.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:03.250
Let's put that aside,

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.330
let's think about a
recurrent neural network.

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:07.490
Hopefully you're all familiar

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:10.530
with the recurrent neural
network, or maybe not anymore.

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.290
I guess everyone is just
using transformer these days.

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:15.110
But recurrent neural
networks are pretty cool.

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:16.720
So let's just look at it very briefly.

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:21.120
A recurrent neural net is
one of the most general form

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.750
of neural network because it is in fact,

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:26.030
a sequential processing machine.

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:29.813
So at time step, T, we
start with the Ht minus one,

00:31:29.813 --> 00:31:32.660
that is the memory state
of the recurrent neural net

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:34.960
at T minus one, so the previous time step.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:38.020
And then we have a recurrent
transition function, G

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:41.490
which often is named as LSTM, or GRU.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:43.700
And then this G is going to take as input

00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:45.460
the previous memories state Ht minus one,

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:47.983
and then the current input Xt,

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:51.273
and computes the next times at Ht.

00:31:52.498 --> 00:31:56.610
Because G is parameterized
with its own progress data.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:58.680
So you can imagine G as a function

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:02.710
that takes as input, Ht
minus one Xt and theta,

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:06.143
in order to produce the newly
updated, hidden state Ht.

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:09.690
So, so far so good?

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:11.550
And then why do we call it
recurring neural network?

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:15.480
Because we're going to use
this same block over and over

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:17.630
as we receive more and more input.

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:19.910
So when we receive the Xt plus one,

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:23.920
G is going to produce Ht plus one.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.530
After that, we're going
to receive Xt plus two,

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:29.010
G is going to produce Ht plus two

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:29.880
and then on and on and on.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.250
And then what you'll notice

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.100
is that these two look exactly the same.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:34.680
And in fact, they have to be same

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:36.970
because I just copied and
pasted one of them to the other.

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:38.450
Then you have to just change the symbols

00:32:38.450 --> 00:32:41.370
and then connected to
more arrows on top of it.

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:43.890
What that means is that we can always view

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:46.470
a kind of interactive
optimization procedure

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:47.380
or the stochastic gradient descent,

00:32:47.380 --> 00:32:49.970
or the gradient based
optimization procedure

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:52.807
as a recurrent neural
network and we solve.

00:32:52.807 --> 00:32:54.830
And then weirdly so,

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.880
once we start looking
at the whole learning

00:32:56.880 --> 00:32:58.360
from this perspective,

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.173
we notice that the gradient
based hyper-parameter tuning

00:33:00.173 --> 00:33:01.680
that we just talked about,

00:33:01.680 --> 00:33:05.440
is equivalent to training
a recurrent neural network

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:08.163
that corresponds to
learning a neural network.

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:13.090
So what that means is
that we have to think

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:15.360
about how to train this
recurrent neural network,

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:17.120
but we're going to take a quick detour

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:21.023
to talk about how interesting,

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:24.367
training a recurrent
neural network can be.

00:33:24.367 --> 00:33:26.130
But before that, there are two questions,

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:28.720
then we try to answer those two questions.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:32.040
So the first question
is from Joao Guillermo,

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.310
how do we choose the
hyper-parameter optimization

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:35.160
hyper-parameters?

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:37.300
Ah, yes, yes.

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:40.700
So, yes are going to
talk about that shortly.

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:42.790
So I'll leave it there.

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:45.740
The other question is from Anwarani,

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:48.580
what are some of the references
on the RNM perspective

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:50.650
for hyper-parameter tuning?

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:52.190
My own paper, I guess.

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:54.060
But, this is nothing new, right?

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:55.550
It's just the same thing

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:57.680
viewed from a slightly different angle.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:00.957
And I talked about it in
my own lecture and so on,

00:34:00.957 --> 00:34:03.240
but I don't think anybody
necessarily wrote down

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:07.033
anything too extensive about
this particular perspective.

00:34:09.910 --> 00:34:12.880
Not everything needs to be
written down as a paper, so.

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:14.393
But anyway, let's continue.

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:18.670
So when you think about
the gradient-based learning

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:20.770
or training of a recurrent neural network,

00:34:21.761 --> 00:34:24.310
we need to think about it from
what the loss function is.

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:25.940
And then for now I'm going to just assume

00:34:25.940 --> 00:34:29.810
that every time step, we take
as the input some observation

00:34:29.810 --> 00:34:31.250
and then we're going to predict something

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:32.950
at that time step.

00:34:32.950 --> 00:34:34.210
And then for that one,

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.640
we have a corresponding
ground truth value.

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:39.793
So we can compute the so-called
per step loss function.

00:34:40.720 --> 00:34:43.140
and then the loss function
for the entire sequence,

00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:46.320
or the entire example is simply the sum

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:49.720
of this per step loss functions.

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:52.400
That's exactly what is shown on the slide.

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:55.250
Then in order to update
the parameter theta

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:57.440
of this recurrent neural network,

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:01.240
we need to compute the
gradient of this total,

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:03.160
let's say, sequence of the loss function

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:05.260
with respect to the
theta that is recurring

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:06.880
on these parameters.

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:08.560
And then we're going to
update the parameters

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:09.970
little by little.

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:12.810
What that means is that
what's most important

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:17.000
is to figure out how to
compute this gradient

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.910
of the sequence of the loss function

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:21.310
with respect to the
recurring of the parameters.

00:35:22.903 --> 00:35:26.150
Now, it turned out the
recurrent neural net

00:35:26.150 --> 00:35:29.060
has a very interesting,
let's say structure

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:30.370
that allows us to compute

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:34.360
this gradient of the
sequence of the loss function

00:35:34.360 --> 00:35:36.310
in one of the two ways.

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:40.610
The first way, I'm going to
just call it future facing

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:45.610
is where we're going to
consider the loss function,

00:35:46.119 --> 00:35:48.390
the gradient of the sequence
of the loss function

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:51.190
with respect to each and every time

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:54.550
the shared parameter theta was used.

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:55.920
So at time step, S,

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:58.070
we are going to use the
shared parameter theta

00:35:58.070 --> 00:36:00.170
by making a copy,

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:03.883
and then use that to
compute the loss function,

00:36:04.820 --> 00:36:07.100
to update the hidden state first,

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:09.050
and then use that to
compute the loss function

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:10.150
at time step, T.

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:12.280
But here because the hidden state

00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:14.088
is going to be using the next time step,

00:36:14.088 --> 00:36:19.088
the influence of this
parameter copy at time step S,

00:36:22.194 --> 00:36:24.697
is going to happen all
the way until the end.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:28.050
So that is what we mean
by the future facing.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:31.170
Every time we use the theta,

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:33.210
the parameters at time step, S

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:36.230
we need to consider
how this parameter copy

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:40.420
is influencing the per step
loss functions in the future.

00:36:40.420 --> 00:36:42.670
That's the reason why we
call it a future facing.

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:44.740
What that means is that
at every time step,

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:46.990
in order for me to compute the gradient

00:36:46.990 --> 00:36:48.400
of the sequence of a loss function,

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:51.970
with respect to the parameter,
used at time step, S,

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:54.610
I need to go forward

00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:59.290
and then back propagate the
error signal through time

00:36:59.290 --> 00:37:00.840
to time step, S.

00:37:00.840 --> 00:37:04.040
So future facing view is a usual way

00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:08.890
to compute the gradient in practice.

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:11.423
That is we use a back
propagation through time.

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:17.140
However, because using
parameters and time step

00:37:17.140 --> 00:37:19.710
and then computing the
per step loss time step

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:20.720
is somewhat dual.

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:25.160
So you can view it from the
bottom up, or from the top down

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.210
you can actually have a past facing view

00:37:27.210 --> 00:37:29.160
of gradient computation as well.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:30.760
That is, we are going to look

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:35.360
at one particular loss
function at time step, T

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:39.220
and then see how all the parameter copies

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:42.420
from time step one, up
until that time step,

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:44.300
and then see how they have influenced

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:47.370
this particular loss
function, at time step, T.

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:50.990
And then to compute that, we
need to look into the past.

00:37:50.990 --> 00:37:53.390
That's why we call it past facing.

00:37:53.390 --> 00:37:55.880
And then this actually
is an interesting view

00:37:55.880 --> 00:38:00.040
because this is not the view
that is most obvious for us,

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.330
like the modern deep
learners, including myself.

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:06.963
Although this was obvious in
'80s and all the '90s, I heard.

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:10.880
So just to summarize these two views,

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:12.610
when we run a recurrent neural net

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:14.890
and then compute the
per step loss function,

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:15.990
and then sum all of them

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:18.390
in order to get the
sequence of a loss function,

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:22.873
there are two ways to
compute this gradient.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:27.790
One is past-facing, that is
that we try to accumulate

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:31.010
the influence of all the past parameters

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:33.790
on the current time steps loss function,

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:36.180
and then sum them all to get the gradient.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:40.120
Or we can go the other way,
that is the future-facing.

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:41.840
At every time we use a parameter,

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.230
we're going to look at
what kind of influence

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:45.700
we're going to have on all
the future loss functions

00:38:45.700 --> 00:38:48.340
and then back propagate
through time, this error signal

00:38:48.340 --> 00:38:50.000
in order to compute the loss function.

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:51.850
And then after that, we sum them all.

00:38:54.020 --> 00:38:55.810
An interesting thing about
the past facing view,

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:59.630
which is in fact the same
thing or similar thing as the-

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:01.840
Probably the same thing as the-

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:02.673
Yeah, same thing.

00:39:02.673 --> 00:39:05.940
Same thing as the fuller mode
of the outer differentiation.

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:08.870
This past-facing view, in fact
that means online learning

00:39:08.870 --> 00:39:10.810
in the case of the
recurrent neural network.

00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:13.290
Because at every time step, T,

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:16.684
we can compute the gradient
of the loss function

00:39:16.684 --> 00:39:18.340
at time step, T

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:22.430
with respect to all the
applications of the theta.

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.730
So we don't need to really look

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:26.940
at what are going to be
received in the future

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:29.690
but we only need to look at
what have been received so far

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:32.120
in order to compute the
gradient of the loss function,

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:33.230
at time step, T,

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:35.300
with respect to the primary applications

00:39:35.300 --> 00:39:38.090
of all the time steps,
which we can just sum,

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:40.220
in order to get the gradient
of the loss function

00:39:40.220 --> 00:39:43.010
at time step, T, with
respect to the parameter.

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:47.610
Now, obviously we just
store all these copies

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:48.470
and then sum it

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:51.830
that becomes the gradient of
the full sequence loss function

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:53.370
with respect to parameters.

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:56.020
But every term in this summation

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:59.003
never needed to wait
for the future inputs.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:01.920
And then the only thing that we need

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.450
in order to turn this
into an online learning

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:06.540
is to update the parameters

00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:11.520
immediately after we
receive one input at a time.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:14.160
So we don't really wait
for the entire sequence

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:15.610
and then update the parameters.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:18.640
But assuming that the
sequence is extremely long

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:20.530
and then relatively stationary,

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:22.020
we're going to just make a small update

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.490
to the parameters as we go forward.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.923
And then this allow us
to update the parameters,

00:40:28.456 --> 00:40:30.480
even before we receive the full sequence.

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:32.630
And if the length of the
sequence is infinite,

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:33.840
then we're going to simply continue

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:36.070
to update the parameters online

00:40:36.070 --> 00:40:40.750
so that the network can adapt
to the varying statistics,

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:42.450
the varying underlying statistics.

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:47.580
Obviously it looks pretty
expensive to compute,

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:49.660
and then it looks like
the amount of the memory

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:52.510
that is necessary is not constant

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:54.070
with respect to the time steps.

00:40:54.070 --> 00:40:56.850
In online learning, if it's not constant

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:59.390
with respect to the
length of the sequence,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:01.320
then it's difficult to say
that it's online learning

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:03.710
because in the infinite limit,

00:41:03.710 --> 00:41:06.200
we're going to require some
infinite amount of the memory

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:09.890
that requires infinite model
compute time for access.

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.820
Of course, we can be a bit relaxing

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:14.790
and say that we were
going to go for the log n

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:15.743
instead of the O1.

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:20.610
But there's still an issue if
the end goal is to infinity.

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:24.140
But it turned out that by
cleverly rearranging the terms

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:28.580
in computing this gradient
of the per step loss function

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:31.690
at times step, T with respect
to the parameters so far,

00:41:31.690 --> 00:41:36.497
we can actually maintain
a constant memory storage

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:40.853
to compute this gradient on the fly.

00:41:41.710 --> 00:41:43.040
I'm not going to go into the detail,

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:46.110
it's just a simple, not
simple, it's very confusing,

00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:49.240
super confusing
rearrangement of the terms,

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:51.350
but eventually all you need to do

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:55.270
is to maintain a
so-called influence matrix

00:41:55.270 --> 00:41:58.050
that tells us about what kind of influence

00:41:58.050 --> 00:42:00.530
all the previous, let's
say, hidden state values

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:03.903
and the parameters have
on the next hidden state.

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:09.590
If I have to check out all those details,

00:42:09.590 --> 00:42:12.010
this so-called real
time recurrent learning

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:14.480
that was proposed by
William &amp; Zipser in 1989,

00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:19.160
becomes a procedure of three lines.

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.760
So we update the influence matrix,

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:23.420
compute the immediate credit

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:25.680
that is that the gradient

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:27.090
of the loss function at time step, T

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:30.770
with respect to the hidden
state at time step, T.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:33.640
And using this, we can
now update the parameters

00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:37.670
using the gradient that
is carried over online.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:38.910
And then we do it over and over

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:41.283
every time we receive a new input.

00:42:43.220 --> 00:42:44.900
It's not widely used in practice,

00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:46.270
that's probably why most of you

00:42:46.270 --> 00:42:47.810
haven't actually heard of it,

00:42:47.810 --> 00:42:51.740
or at least haven't used it,
even though you knew about it

00:42:51.740 --> 00:42:53.780
because it's still quite expensive.

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:56.890
In particular, the issue is
with the memory complexity.

00:42:56.890 --> 00:43:00.000
It's often considered this ON cubed

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:01.660
where the N is the number of neurons.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:03.680
So we're going to just
consider a single layer,

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:06.360
recurrent neural network,
and is fully connected.

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:07.870
So we have the N neurons

00:43:07.870 --> 00:43:09.493
and then the number of
the waves is N by N,

00:43:09.493 --> 00:43:11.027
so that is N squared.

00:43:11.027 --> 00:43:14.880
And as I said, what this
influence matrix is carrying over

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:18.088
is what kind of influence the hidden state

00:43:18.088 --> 00:43:19.420
at time step T minus one

00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:22.180
and the weight matrix is going to have

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:23.863
on hidden state at time step, T.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:26.270
Of course you can rearrange some of them,

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:28.950
but still you end up
with the N cubed number

00:43:28.950 --> 00:43:31.903
of the entries in this influence matrix.

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:35.270
But obviously, what that also means

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:38.160
is that if the number of parameters

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.670
of this recurrent neural net

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:43.160
was constant with respect
to the number of neurons,

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:45.350
then suddenly this
real-time recurrent learning

00:43:45.350 --> 00:43:50.310
is a very viable and perhaps
even preferable solution

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:52.040
to train our recurrent neural network,

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:54.280
because we don't have to wait all the way

00:43:54.280 --> 00:43:55.730
until the end of the sequence

00:43:56.710 --> 00:43:59.180
in order to update the
parameters just once.

00:43:59.180 --> 00:44:01.230
As we read the long sequences,

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:03.610
we can just update the
parameters on the fly.

00:44:03.610 --> 00:44:08.190
Effectively, the ratio of the
number of updates per sequence

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:13.190
becomes let's say, is
significantly greater.

00:44:13.770 --> 00:44:15.940
So perhaps in this particular case,

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:17.540
this is going to be very useful.

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:22.380
So we have learned how to
train our recurrent neural net

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:23.450
in an online manner.

00:44:23.450 --> 00:44:25.390
Is there any questions on this

00:44:25.390 --> 00:44:27.950
before I talk about online
hyper-parameter tuning,

00:44:27.950 --> 00:44:29.720
which is the meat of this lecture,

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:31.320
except that there is a very thin meat,

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:34.190
we're going to just cover it very quickly.

00:44:34.190 --> 00:44:36.280
So I only see Joao's question

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:38.560
that was from the
previous, let's say break.

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:40.850
Yeah, this one I'm
going to get to it soon.

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:43.430
The answer is that it's a bit difficult,

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:45.540
but I'll talk about it shortly.

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:47.430
Okay, otherwise, I'm going to it.

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:49.710
Now at this point, it becomes a bit

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:54.400
let's say relatively
straightforward to see already

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:56.033
what I'm going to end up doing.

00:44:57.214 --> 00:44:59.913
What we notice is that
we know how to train

00:44:59.913 --> 00:45:03.040
a recurrent neural
network in online manner

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:04.580
and we noticed that learning

00:45:04.580 --> 00:45:06.079
with the stochastic gradient descent

00:45:06.079 --> 00:45:08.040
or any kind of iterative optimization,

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:11.360
is equivalent to running the
recurring neural net once.

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:13.850
So then we're going to combine these two

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:15.380
so that we're going to update

00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:17.900
the optimization
hyper-parameters on the fly.

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:21.040
As soon as I said this, there
are a couple of questions.

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:23.090
Let me try to answer that before I go on.

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:27.060
So the anonymous attendee ask a question.

