00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:26.480
So what that means is that we can use

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:28.500
this real time recurrent learning

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:30.430
for training our recurrent neural net

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:32.240
for the purpose of optimizing

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:34.980
or tuning the optimization
of hyper-parameters,

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:37.120
as we train our neural network

00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:38.570
using stochastic gradient descent

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:40.070
because training a neural net

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:41.960
with the stochastic gradient descent

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:46.113
is equivalent to running
recurrent neural net once forward.

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:51.230
Now it turned out

00:47:51.230 --> 00:47:54.090
that we have some
expensive computation here

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:56.590
because we should be able
to compute the Jacobian

00:47:56.590 --> 00:47:59.850
of the hidden state of
the recurrent neural net

00:47:59.850 --> 00:48:02.167
that is the primary setup
of the neural network.

00:48:02.167 --> 00:48:04.310
So the network parameters at time step, T

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:06.930
with respect to the network
parameter at T minus one.

00:48:06.930 --> 00:48:08.970
It's a gigantic Hessian.

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:11.050
Again, we don't have to
compute it explicitly,

00:48:11.050 --> 00:48:13.100
but we should be able
to compute this Hessian,

00:48:13.100 --> 00:48:16.860
the actual product there
or the gradient Hessian

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:22.203
so that the Hessian and
Jacobian product there.

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:24.120
It's still expensive.

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:26.930
But imagine how it was before,

00:48:26.930 --> 00:48:29.390
we had to compute this
Hessian vector product

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:32.840
as many times as we applied the S3D update

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:36.020
to the neural network for updating

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:37.010
the hyper parameter once.

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:38.840
But here we just do it once

00:48:38.840 --> 00:48:40.740
every time we update the hyper-parameter.

00:48:40.740 --> 00:48:42.540
So this is much more doable,

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:46.048
although it's quite expensive
for a very large network.

00:48:46.048 --> 00:48:48.490
And it turned out that for
this particular purpose,

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:50.050
a stochastic approximation

00:48:50.050 --> 00:48:51.830
or the stochastic finite
difference approximation

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:54.840
to this Hessian vector
product works pretty well.

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:57.330
Check out this blog post
by Domke, Justin Domke

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:02.670
it's a really concise but very extensive

00:49:02.670 --> 00:49:04.330
let's say, discussion
on what is the right way

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:05.930
to do this kind of
finite difference method

00:49:05.930 --> 00:49:08.080
to compute the Hessian
vector product well.

00:49:09.350 --> 00:49:11.820
So yes, it is computation on the feasible.

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:12.653
Why is it that?

00:49:12.653 --> 00:49:14.590
Because often the number of
the hyper-parameters we have

00:49:14.590 --> 00:49:17.417
is significantly smaller
than the number of parameters

00:49:17.417 --> 00:49:19.050
and the network size.

00:49:19.050 --> 00:49:20.970
And then we often make sure

00:49:20.970 --> 00:49:23.300
that the number of
hyper-parameters is independent

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:24.800
from the size of the network.

00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:27.740
What that means is that
it's not really ON cubed

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:30.430
but it's ON with a very small constant,

00:49:30.430 --> 00:49:33.850
that is lambda squared,
that is in front of this N.

00:49:33.850 --> 00:49:35.590
And then assuming that the
number of hyper-parameters

00:49:35.590 --> 00:49:37.570
is small enough it's okay.

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:41.670
And even if we want to use it

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:45.010
with a lot of hyper
parameters, we can always go

00:49:45.010 --> 00:49:47.510
for computationally cheaper
unbiased approximations,

00:49:47.510 --> 00:49:49.680
such as UORO, Unbiased
Online Recurrent Optimization

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:51.943
by Tallec &amp; Ollivier, 2017.

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:55.500
So relatively straightforward.

00:49:55.500 --> 00:49:58.580
Again, if you have checked out all these,

00:49:58.580 --> 00:50:01.560
let's say low-level details
or the gory details,

00:50:01.560 --> 00:50:02.940
let me put it like that.

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:06.740
Then it's a three line
or three step procedure.

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:09.020
We update the influence matrix,

00:50:09.020 --> 00:50:11.290
we compute the immediate credit,

00:50:11.290 --> 00:50:13.280
and then we just update
the hyper-parameters

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:16.097
and we do it every time
we receive a new input.

00:50:16.097 --> 00:50:17.320
And then what is the new input here?

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:21.183
As I receive a new mini
batch of training examples.

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:24.090
So before I go into the experiment,

00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:26.260
there's two major properties.

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:28.240
First one is that fully online.

00:50:28.240 --> 00:50:30.880
We really literally update
the network parameters

00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:34.190
and optimization
hyper-parameters simultaneously.

00:50:34.190 --> 00:50:36.490
We don't like, let's say,

00:50:36.490 --> 00:50:39.220
let's take a look at the
few more mini batches

00:50:39.220 --> 00:50:41.890
and then go forward and them
backward, we don't do that.

00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:45.730
We update these two like simultaneously.

00:50:45.730 --> 00:50:47.800
Every time we update
the network parameter,

00:50:47.800 --> 00:50:50.470
we can update the
optimization hyper-parameters.

00:50:50.470 --> 00:50:51.950
Of course, if you want,

00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:54.840
we can always make them
asynchronous, if you want.

00:50:54.840 --> 00:50:58.900
But, generally we just update
both of them synchronously.

00:50:58.900 --> 00:51:01.020
And then the second property
is that it's almost exact.

00:51:01.020 --> 00:51:03.970
With a small enough meta-learning
rate, it is almost exact.

00:51:08.427 --> 00:51:11.340
We actually wrote a nice paper about this,

00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:14.290
I put it on archives, submitted to ICML.

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:18.133
Well, and then the reviewers
gracefully pointed out

00:51:18.133 --> 00:51:20.650
that we weren't the first
to come up with this idea.

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:22.580
Of course, unsurprisingly,
there was a paper

00:51:22.580 --> 00:51:26.000
from Luca Franceschi et al, 2017

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:28.170
where they talk about
this particular algorithm

00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:30.680
in one section of the paper.

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:33.430
And then I looked at the
paper, thanks to our reviewers

00:51:33.430 --> 00:51:36.180
and then noticed that it's
exactly the same idea.

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:38.930
The idea itself is
really not that dramatic

00:51:38.930 --> 00:51:40.230
or groundbreaking

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:42.503
it's just how we view the whole problem.

00:51:43.796 --> 00:51:45.730
That's what kind of made it really fun.

00:51:45.730 --> 00:51:48.330
but it turns out it's the same thing.

00:51:48.330 --> 00:51:50.890
Except that they run only one experiment

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:54.300
but because we didn't
know that Luca did it,

00:51:54.300 --> 00:51:56.590
we kind of ran a very
extensive set of experiments

00:51:56.590 --> 00:52:00.110
to verify that this idea
actually works pretty well

00:52:00.110 --> 00:52:01.483
in practice as well.

00:52:02.350 --> 00:52:04.840
So let's go into the experimental details.

00:52:04.840 --> 00:52:05.890
We have only five minutes,

00:52:05.890 --> 00:52:07.090
so I'm going to just finish it off

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:08.490
and then get some questions.

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:12.940
So we ran an extensive set of experiments,

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:15.660
although, on a small,
very small scale data sets

00:52:15.660 --> 00:52:17.260
MNIST and CIFAR-10.

00:52:17.260 --> 00:52:18.970
And then we use a four-layer,

00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:20.260
fully-connected neural net for MNIST

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:23.330
and then we used ResNet-18 for CIFAR-10.

00:52:23.330 --> 00:52:25.240
We used Adam optimizer,

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:28.330
although we did run the same
set of experiments with S3D

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:32.060
without much of the difference
between these two optimizers.

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:34.700
So first of all, does it actually work?

00:52:34.700 --> 00:52:36.950
If I compare it to the
blackbox optimization,

00:52:37.820 --> 00:52:40.720
can this online hyper-parameter tuning

00:52:40.720 --> 00:52:44.410
get to the test loss
or the validation loss

00:52:44.410 --> 00:52:46.760
that is as good as the
lowest that we can find

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:50.480
using the blackbox
optimization in one sweep?

00:52:50.480 --> 00:52:51.350
Not even a sweep, right?

00:52:51.350 --> 00:52:53.070
It's just one training run.

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:54.950
So we compared it against a random search,

00:52:54.950 --> 00:52:57.770
as well as the Bayesian
optimization with a scikit-opt.

00:52:57.770 --> 00:53:01.600
And then we ran it until
the target test loss

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:03.900
went below 0.3.

00:53:03.900 --> 00:53:06.010
And then it turned out
that what be proposed,

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:07.260
okay, so we shouldn't really,

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:08.940
I shouldn't really say propose any more.

00:53:08.940 --> 00:53:11.440
But anyway, this online
hyper-parameter optimization

00:53:11.440 --> 00:53:12.277
based on R3RL

00:53:13.160 --> 00:53:16.010
we're able to find or hit this target

00:53:16.010 --> 00:53:17.810
in just one training run.

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:21.150
While for blackbox
optimization algorithms,

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:22.650
obviously couldn't do it in one run.

00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:24.220
I mean, it just doesn't make any sense

00:53:24.220 --> 00:53:25.660
unless you get super lucky,

00:53:25.660 --> 00:53:30.330
and it require many,
many if not tens of runs.

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:33.573
So this actually told us
that it actually works.

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:38.190
And then stability-wise, this is connected

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:39.023
to how to choose

00:53:39.023 --> 00:53:40.763
a hyper-parameter
optimization hyper-parameters.

00:53:40.763 --> 00:53:41.596
And then,

00:53:41.596 --> 00:53:44.760
one of the hyper-parameter
optimization hyper-parameters

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:46.580
is the initialization.

00:53:46.580 --> 00:53:50.130
So how do we initialize the
optimization hyper-parameters?

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:52.490
And then do we have to
be very careful about it?

00:53:52.490 --> 00:53:54.140
As soon as we have to be careful about it,

00:53:54.140 --> 00:53:57.470
or use the utility of this approach

00:53:57.470 --> 00:53:59.668
kind of vanishes largely.

00:53:59.668 --> 00:54:02.830
So, what we did was we randomly vary

00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:03.777
the initial learning rate,

00:54:03.777 --> 00:54:06.250
and we randomly vary the
regularization coefficient.

00:54:06.250 --> 00:54:08.607
We randomly vary scheduling coefficients

00:54:08.607 --> 00:54:10.300
for the algorithm and whatnot,

00:54:10.300 --> 00:54:13.650
and also the initialization
of this learning rate

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:15.450
and regularization coefficients.

00:54:15.450 --> 00:54:19.510
And then what we have noticed
is that in almost all cases

00:54:19.510 --> 00:54:23.510
within one run, it was able
to get to a very low test loss

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:25.780
with a very small variance,

00:54:25.780 --> 00:54:29.653
which was very interesting and assuring.

00:54:31.830 --> 00:54:35.340
And what was most as
exciting, at least to me,

00:54:35.340 --> 00:54:38.690
was that as we increased the
number of hyper-parameters.

00:54:38.690 --> 00:54:40.600
So what we did was that we started

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:43.950
by using one initial learning rate,

00:54:43.950 --> 00:54:46.420
or the one learning rate
for the entire network.

00:54:46.420 --> 00:54:50.870
We started to divide
our network layer-wise

00:54:50.870 --> 00:54:53.030
and then you introduce more
and more learning rate.

00:54:53.030 --> 00:54:54.350
And then what we saw was that, of course,

00:54:54.350 --> 00:54:57.800
the wall clock time increase
as the theory predicts,

00:54:57.800 --> 00:55:01.680
but at the same time,
the resulting test loss

00:55:01.680 --> 00:55:04.280
decreased almost monotonically.

00:55:04.280 --> 00:55:06.850
Which is a extremely good indication

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.820
that this is in fact solving

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:11.770
this optimization problem correctly.

00:55:11.770 --> 00:55:15.260
Because more theory of freedom,
better solution refines

00:55:15.260 --> 00:55:17.940
this has to be rather indirect

00:55:17.940 --> 00:55:22.940
but still a very convincing
evidence that this optimization

00:55:23.150 --> 00:55:25.780
actually is solving optimization problems.

00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:28.220
And obviously one of the
things that online learning

00:55:28.220 --> 00:55:30.730
should be able to do so is the adaptation.

00:55:30.730 --> 00:55:32.940
So as the underlying let's say, problem

00:55:32.940 --> 00:55:35.220
is perturbed or goes through a change

00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:37.530
or goes through a distributional shift,

00:55:37.530 --> 00:55:41.063
will it be able to
recover or adapt rapidly?

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:43.910
We don't really have a
good, let's say benchmark

00:55:43.910 --> 00:55:46.730
to do about test this setup.

00:55:46.730 --> 00:55:47.563
So what we did

00:55:47.563 --> 00:55:51.520
was to artificially perturb
the hyper-parameters

00:55:51.520 --> 00:55:53.340
in the middle of training,

00:55:53.340 --> 00:55:56.780
and then we saw if this online
hyper-parameter optimization

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:59.680
can recover from such perturbation.

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:02.140
Turned out, that it can
really do so pretty well

00:56:02.140 --> 00:56:03.363
and pretty rapidly.

00:56:05.120 --> 00:56:07.250
So, because I really have only one minute,

00:56:07.250 --> 00:56:09.630
so I'm going to actually pass this.

00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:12.590
Now, one thing that I want
to really emphasize here

00:56:12.590 --> 00:56:14.910
is that there is a big question

00:56:14.910 --> 00:56:17.720
and then it connects to the
discussion very early on

00:56:17.720 --> 00:56:20.320
is that does choice of
the hyper-parameters

00:56:20.320 --> 00:56:22.560
really matter in the long run?

00:56:22.560 --> 00:56:25.420
If I start with a very large
learning rate in the beginning,

00:56:25.420 --> 00:56:26.850
how much does that actually influence

00:56:26.850 --> 00:56:28.160
the final solution I find?

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:29.900
If I start with a very high,

00:56:29.900 --> 00:56:31.850
let's say regularization
coefficients at the beginning,

00:56:31.850 --> 00:56:34.850
how does it actually
influence the future solution

00:56:34.850 --> 00:56:36.250
that I will get after training

00:56:36.250 --> 00:56:38.330
for let's say hundreds of thousands,

00:56:38.330 --> 00:56:40.210
if not millions of updates.

00:56:40.210 --> 00:56:42.410
And then if there is such thing,

00:56:42.410 --> 00:56:44.930
and then that can only be captured

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:47.210
if the optimization algorithm
is able to capture it.

00:56:47.210 --> 00:56:48.880
And then the real-time recurrent learning

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.260
does not use the
information from the future.

00:56:51.260 --> 00:56:53.690
So can it actually carry
over all those information

00:56:53.690 --> 00:56:56.480
to be used in the future
without knowing the future?

00:56:56.480 --> 00:56:58.220
It turned out that the answer is yes.

00:56:58.220 --> 00:57:01.710
If you reset this influence
matrix over and over,

00:57:01.710 --> 00:57:04.890
then the resulting test
loss is always higher.

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:08.620
And then the best case
loss could only be obtained

00:57:08.620 --> 00:57:10.950
only when we never reset
the influence matrix,

00:57:10.950 --> 00:57:13.840
but carry over all the
information that is out there.

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:15.880
So it was a very interesting and exciting

00:57:15.880 --> 00:57:18.100
with the observation we made.

00:57:18.100 --> 00:57:19.670
So I'm going to skip through it

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:20.580
because there are a few questions

00:57:20.580 --> 00:57:22.690
that I want to answer as well.

00:57:22.690 --> 00:57:25.080
So one of the reasons why I decided

00:57:25.080 --> 00:57:26.720
to talk about this at this summer school

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:29.660
is that it's not only
about trying to use a setup

00:57:29.660 --> 00:57:32.270
that has been proposed or has
been used by the community,

00:57:32.270 --> 00:57:34.610
but trying to see the same problem

00:57:34.610 --> 00:57:36.050
from a very different perspective,

00:57:36.050 --> 00:57:37.990
and then trying to come
up with a new framework

00:57:37.990 --> 00:57:39.750
and then based on the new perspective,

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:41.460
often you can actually come
up with a new algorithm

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:43.580
that is not necessarily too complicated

00:57:43.580 --> 00:57:46.060
or too groundbreaking, but just a clever,

00:57:46.060 --> 00:57:48.400
well, it's weird to call
my algorithm clever,

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:50.710
but let's say the interesting
and fun, let's say way

00:57:50.710 --> 00:57:52.410
to reuse many of the ideas

00:57:52.410 --> 00:57:55.100
and acquire them from the same field

00:57:55.100 --> 00:57:56.490
as well as the adjacent field.

00:57:56.490 --> 00:57:57.670
So it was really fun.

00:57:57.670 --> 00:57:58.990
We were able to make connection

00:57:58.990 --> 00:58:00.490
between the recurrent neural net,

00:58:00.490 --> 00:58:01.497
stochastic gradient descent

00:58:01.497 --> 00:58:03.390
and the online learning in this work

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:06.190
to get to this online
hyper-parameter optimization.

00:58:06.190 --> 00:58:08.210
There are a few things
that needs to be considered

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:09.960
with studying the future.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:13.230
One is that this one probably

00:58:13.230 --> 00:58:14.950
should overfit to a validation loss,

00:58:14.950 --> 00:58:17.604
but we cannot actually see the overfits

00:58:17.604 --> 00:58:18.503
to the validation loss.

00:58:18.503 --> 00:58:20.840
Apparently, I think there is
a weird, let's say interaction

00:58:20.840 --> 00:58:22.371
between minimizing the validation loss

00:58:22.371 --> 00:58:24.020
and minimizing on the training loss.

00:58:24.020 --> 00:58:25.820
We have to work on that one.

00:58:25.820 --> 00:58:27.490
The second thing is
that the online learning

00:58:27.490 --> 00:58:29.400
is only as good as you're
going to be working

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:31.190
with non-stationary environments.

00:58:31.190 --> 00:58:33.830
And then we'll see how it works

00:58:33.830 --> 00:58:35.520
in the actual non-stationary environments,

00:58:35.520 --> 00:58:39.000
where the data sets shifts,
the distribution shifts

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:41.420
with the infinite stream of the data.

00:58:41.420 --> 00:58:45.480
And then final thing is that
we use a lot of different,

00:58:45.480 --> 00:58:46.870
let's say hyper-parameters,

00:58:46.870 --> 00:58:48.880
and then your idea was working okay,

00:58:48.880 --> 00:58:50.650
but still as a hyper-parameter,

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:52.810
such as a meta-learning rate grows,

00:58:52.810 --> 00:58:55.040
the inexact nature of the
outcome grows as well.

00:58:55.040 --> 00:58:57.450
So what does that actually imply?

00:58:57.450 --> 00:58:58.540
The kind of thing-

00:58:58.540 --> 00:58:59.870
We need to think about it.

00:58:59.870 --> 00:59:01.450
Finally, final slide.

00:59:01.450 --> 00:59:04.140
So last week at ICML, one
of the outstanding papers

00:59:04.140 --> 00:59:08.720
was given to Vicol,
Metz and Sohl-Dickstein

00:59:08.720 --> 00:59:10.340
titled, "Unbiased Gradient Estimation

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:11.650
in Unrolled Computation Graphs

00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:13.250
with Persistent Evolution Strategies".

00:59:13.250 --> 00:59:15.320
This is really interesting paper to read.

00:59:15.320 --> 00:59:18.430
I still need to read it more
carefully and then digest it,

00:59:18.430 --> 00:59:20.980
but they actually approach this problem

00:59:20.980 --> 00:59:24.230
of their online optimization
hyper-parameter,

00:59:24.230 --> 00:59:27.250
and then mix it together with
the evolutionary algorithm

00:59:27.250 --> 00:59:30.790
or more of the gradient
free optimization algorithm.

00:59:30.790 --> 00:59:32.840
And then they show that
you can actually come up

00:59:32.840 --> 00:59:34.593
with the unbiased approach to this.

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:39.190
Still, I always had this thing about,

00:59:39.190 --> 00:59:42.730
oh, well if I tend to the
gradient free optimization,

00:59:42.730 --> 00:59:45.000
why don't I just use a
gradient and make it better?

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:46.830
But it looks like a very cool paper.

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:47.790
And then if you're interested

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:49.770
in this online
hyper-parameter optimization,

00:59:49.770 --> 00:59:54.770
I highly recommend you to
check this out as well.

00:59:54.950 --> 00:59:56.420
Well, that actually concludes my talk,

00:59:56.420 --> 00:59:58.130
but let me try to answer a few questions

00:59:58.130 --> 01:00:01.000
that are in the QA panel.

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:03.300
Joao, hopefully I have
answered your question

01:00:03.300 --> 01:00:06.710
so very, very stable
within a reasonable range.

01:00:06.710 --> 01:00:08.910
But we probably need to get a better sense

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:11.730
of what the reasonable range is,

01:00:11.730 --> 01:00:14.310
both theoretically and empirically.

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:16.710
Next question from Bradley Brigham,

01:00:16.710 --> 01:00:19.173
does one run of OHO take
significantly more steps

01:00:19.173 --> 01:00:20.870
